family: M1
created-by: t
populated-by: t
operated-by: p
op: read-metadata read-content checkout-copy tar-export static-delta-apply
modes: archive bare-user
tier: T0
outcome: full
severity: interop
identity: full
oracle: manifest checksum-agreement
evidence: ostrya::read, ostrya::read_modes, ostrya::checkout, ostrya::tar,
 ostrya::delta
spec: format-reference.md#object-store-layout
note: These are the paths the checked-in golden fixtures already exercise: the
 port reads the tool's archive tree and bare-user tarball, imports the tool's tar
 export, and applies a tool-generated delta.

family: M1
created-by: t
populated-by: t
operated-by: p
op: lock-exclusion
modes: archive bare bare-user bare-user-only
tier: T0
outcome: full
severity: interop
identity: n-a
oracle: exit-status
evidence: port-plan.md decision 8
spec: port-plan.md#decisions
note: The port takes a classic fcntl record lock, which shares a lock space with
 the tool's OFD locks, so the two exclude each other. Verified by holding each
 side's lock while the other acquires.

family: M1
created-by: t
populated-by: t
operated-by: p
op: fsck diff config-read commit-into refs-update prune summary-regenerate
 sign-verify static-delta-generate
modes: archive bare-user
tier: T0
outcome: unobserved
severity: interop
identity: unobserved
oracle: exit-status checksum-agreement inventory
evidence: -
question: The port has all of these operations in its library, and the existing
 tests compare them against the tool on repositories the port itself built. Run
 them against a repository the tool created and populated, and confirm the
 uniform postcondition: the tool still fscks the repository clean afterwards.

